8
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:09,080
First of all, we need to understand what
an interrupt is and what its purpose is

9
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:16,119
in programming and microcontrollers.
Let me give you a simple example.

10
00:01:16,119 --> 00:01:23,960
In the previous sessions I told you that
the CPU's job is to execute the program.

11
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:32,600
The CPU is responsible for executing the program.
Now, when we write a program,

12
00:01:32,600 --> 00:01:39,600
how is it executed? When we program the
microcontroller, the program starts

13
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:45,200
from the top and is executed line by line,
moving downward.

14
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:51,600
It continues from the top to the bottom
in order.

15
00:01:51,600 --> 00:02:00,600
Now, in the middle of the program, suppose
I write an instruction saying that if a switch

16
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:08,239
or push button is pressed, it should
toggle an LED or perform some other action.

17
00:02:08,239 --> 00:02:14,560
But perhaps I do not press that switch
or push button for ten days.

18
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:20,160
Every time the program runs from top to bottom,
it still has to check it,

19
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:25,440
to see whether the switch has been pressed
or not before doing the required action.

20
00:02:25,440 --> 00:02:30,840
Then it continues executing the next line,
and again, from the top,

21
00:02:30,840 --> 00:02:36,360
when it reaches this point, it checks again
whether the switch has been pressed,

22
00:02:36,360 --> 00:02:45,879
and so on. This process can become so time-consuming
that it is not efficient at all.

23
00:02:45,879 --> 00:02:55,239
This is where the role of the interrupt becomes
important. How does it work?

24
00:02:55,239 --> 00:02:59,519
I use an interrupt and configure one of the pins
as an external interrupt.

25
00:02:59,519 --> 00:03:08,200
Now, when the program runs from top to bottom,
it no longer needs to check that line.

26
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:14,680
That line is handled by the interrupt,
while the following lines continue to execute.

27
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:23,760
The other lines continue to be checked
from top to bottom, and only the part

28
00:03:23,760 --> 00:03:31,360
related to the event is placed inside
a function called a callback,

29
00:03:31,360 --> 00:03:38,879
which I will explain later. When the switch
or push button is pressed, at that moment

30
00:03:38,879 --> 00:03:47,239
the CPU spends its time handling that event.
So, as I said, interrupts have a very

31
00:03:47,239 --> 00:03:53,319
important role in timing and other applications.
I will explain their features in the next slide.

32
00:03:53,319 --> 00:04:00,680
So, the concept of an interrupt
is basically this:

33
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:05,799
a program normally runs by polling;
it executes line by line from top to bottom,

34
00:04:05,799 --> 00:04:12,400
and when it finishes, it starts again from the top.
If there is no interrupt,

35
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,759
it keeps checking an instruction that we may
hardly ever need,

36
00:04:16,759 --> 00:04:21,799
even if we have no use for it for a long time.
With an interrupt, we define it

37
00:04:21,799 --> 00:04:30,759
so that it does not have to keep checking;
it is checked only when the event actually occurs,

38
00:04:31,520 --> 00:04:36,360
for example, when a push button is pressed.

39
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:42,960
So, without an interrupt, the processor,
or CPU, has to keep checking that line,

40
00:04:42,960 --> 00:04:48,720
but with an interrupt, when the event occurs,
the CPU handles it,

41
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:52,800
so the program becomes faster,
more organized, and lower-power.

42
00:04:53,199 --> 00:04:59,479
For today's project, which we are going to
demonstrate both in programming and in practice,

43
