74
00:08:22,280 --> 00:08:30,440
Now let's see what rising edge and falling edge
mean. When we say rising edge,

75
00:08:30,440 --> 00:08:38,719
it means the signal is increasing:
it starts at 0 and then moves to 1.

76
00:08:38,719 --> 00:08:45,480
That is the signal we define.
And when we say falling edge,

77
00:08:45,480 --> 00:08:51,720
it means that when the switch is pressed,
the pin changes state.

78
00:08:51,720 --> 00:08:57,279
The pin goes from 1 to 0 because it is
falling, meaning it moves downward.

79
00:08:57,279 --> 00:09:04,279
So it was at 1 and changes to 0.
Keep in mind that we have these two states:

80
00:09:04,279 --> 00:09:10,600
rising edge and falling edge.
Based on these, we can define our pull-up or pull-down.

81
00:09:10,600 --> 00:09:16,760
For example, if we use a falling edge,
meaning the signal falls from high to low,

82
00:09:16,760 --> 00:09:23,160
we should use a pull-up, because the input
will be pulled down to 0 when the button is pressed.

83
00:09:23,160 --> 00:09:29,600
And when we use a rising edge, meaning
a rising transition triggers the interrupt,

84
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:36,880
we should use a pull-down arrangement.
Why? Because the signal rises from 0 to 1,

85
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:43,560
so its initial value should be 0.
Now, in the project, I configured it this way.

86
00:09:43,560 --> 00:09:51,079
